Lithium Drums: fix JUCE 9.0.4's Linux drag-out (performExternalDragDropOfFiles)
under XWayland, which is how every JUCE app runs on a Wayland desktop.
Applied by cmake/ApplyPatch.cmake from cmake/Dependencies.cmake.

1. Finding the drop target. JUCE followed XQueryPointer's child chain from the
   root. Under XWayland that chain is empty once the pointer leaves the app's
   own windows (even over another X11 app), so no target was ever found. Now,
   when the chain finds nothing, the target is picked from the pointer's root
   position and the stacking order (as GTK and Qt do), and failing that the
   compositor's XDnD proxy (GNOME maps one over the Wayland surface under the
   pointer, but in logical coordinates, so with display scaling it isn't where
   the position test looks).
2. Answering TARGETS. GNOME's X11-to-Wayland bridge asks the XdndSelection
   owner for TARGETS before anything else. JUCE refused, so the bridge had no
   type to offer the Wayland app and the drop delivered nothing.
3. Percent-encoding the file URIs. "file://" + path left spaces raw, which
   makes an invalid text/uri-list entry that file managers reject.
4. Leaving a target for nothing. targetWindow was kept, so the old target got
   an XdndLeave on every pointer move. It's now cleared. The type list is also
   no longer added to again on every drag.
5. Ending the drag. XdndSelection was never given up, and GNOME keeps its side
   of an X11-to-Wayland drag going while the source owns it, which could leave
   the desktop not taking clicks. It's released when the drag ends. A drop the
   target never answers with XdndFinished no longer blocks later drags forever.

